\section{Introduction}
Wagi (ISO 639-3: fad; Glottocode wagi1249) is a Trans-New Guinea language of Madang Province, Papua New Guinea, also recorded under the names Kamba, Kauris, Silibob and Mis Furan. Sources give about 3{,}400 speakers (2003) and rate it as vigorous. Searches found only a small amount of material; no school primers, textbooks, or dictionaries were found, and none are claimed here.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No primers, textbooks or school materials in Wagi were found in search results.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Wikipedia overview of the Wagi language: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wagi_language}
\item Trans New Guinea database entry for Spencer et al.\ 2011, a SIL sociolinguistic survey of the Wagi varieties (Kamba, Silibob, Foran, Kauris, Mis): \url{https://transnewguinea.org/source/spencer-et-al-2011?page=3}
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
Global Recordings Network lists Christian videos, Bibles and songs linked for Wagi, but I did not inspect the individual items: \url{https://globalrecordings.net/en/language/fad}

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Wikitongues language page for Wagi (vitality rating and community-submitted material, if any): \url{https://wikitongues.org/languages/fad/}
\item Global Recordings Network reported no audio recordings in Wagi at the time of the search (see link above).
\end{itemize}

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
Wagi is used by all adults and many but not all young people, so a mother-tongue curriculum would have to be built largely from scratch, starting from an orthography and the SIL survey findings on dialect variation. No existing school programme was found.

