\section{Introduction}
Fagani (ISO 639-3: faf; Glottocode faga1239) is an Austronesian (San Cristobal) language of northwest Makira, Solomon Islands. Wikipedia gives about 900 speakers (a 1999 figure) and lists the dialects Agufi, Fagani and Rihu'a; UNESCO classifies it as vulnerable. Search turned up very little beyond general reference pages.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No primers, textbooks or school materials in Fagani were found in search results.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Wikipedia overview of the Fagani language (mentions Arthur Capell materials held at Paradisec): \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fagani_language}
\item Verbix language page for Fagani: \url{https://docs.verbix.com/Languages/Fagani}
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
Joshua Project's page for Fagani in Solomon Islands reports a Christian majority and says Bible portions exist, but not a full New Testament: \url{https://joshuaproject.net/people_groups/11176}

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
No audio, video or software resources were found in search results.

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
There is no known school use of Fagani. Any curriculum would need to begin with locating the Capell/Paradisec holdings and any Bible portions, and with community consultation on orthography and speaker numbers, since the available figures are dated.

