\date{}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Finongan (ISO 639-3: fag; Glottocode fino1238), also called Finungwa or Finungwan, is a Finisterre-branch Trans-New Guinea language of the Sarawaged Range, Morobe Province, Papua New Guinea. Speaker estimates range from about 1{,}100 to 2{,}600 depending on source and date; Wikitongues rates it vigorous. Villages named include Bakame, Kalange, Gifan and Mama.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No primers or textbooks were found in search results.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Webonary Finongan dictionary project, with a foreword describing the dialects (Bakame/Kalange basis; Mama differs; Gifan mixed): \url{https://www.webonary.work/finongan/overview/foreword/}
\item Wikipedia overview of Finongan: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Finongan_language}
\item Counting-system study page (Smith 1988, via the MPI EVA numerals database): \url{https://lingweb.eva.mpg.de/channumerals/Finongan.htm}
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
Joshua Project (listed as Finungwan) reports about 90\% Christian and Bible portions but no complete New Testament: \url{https://joshuaproject.net/people_groups/11210}

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Global Recordings Network page for Finongan (contents not inspected): \url{https://globalrecordings.net/en/language/fag}
\item Wikitongues page for Finongan: \url{https://wikitongues.org/languages/fag/}
\end{itemize}

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
Finongan is used by all community members, which favours mother-tongue instruction, but no school use was found. The Webonary dictionary is the most useful starting point for vocabulary and orthography; its coverage and licence should be checked directly.

\end{document}
