\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Fali of Baissa (ISO 639-3: fah; Glottocode bais1242) is an unclassified Benue-Congo language once spoken around Baissa, Taraba State, Nigeria. Wikipedia describes it as extinct; the Endangered Languages Project, citing Roger Blench's Atlas of Nigerian Languages (2012), reports that a few individuals may still speak it on the Falinga Plateau, with low certainty. No current speaker count was found.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
None found. No teaching materials, primers or school programmes were found in search results.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Wikipedia entry: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fali_of_Baissa}
\item Endangered Languages Project entry, with Blench (2012) information: \url{https://endangeredlanguages.com/lang/4144?hl=en}
\item Endangered Languages Project bibliography for the language: \url{https://endangeredlanguages.com/lang/4144/bibliography}
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
None found.

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
An Endangered Languages Project samples page exists for this language, but I did not inspect its contents: \url{https://www.endangeredlanguages.com/lang/4144/samples/9554?hl=es}

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
Documentation appears minimal, so a heritage or revitalization curriculum would first depend on fieldwork with any remaining speakers or descendants on the Falinga Plateau and on locating the sources in the ELP bibliography. Claims about the language's status should be treated as uncertain.

\end{document}
